# PHASE 9 — ACCESSIBILITY AND PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION

Improve the entire website without compromising the existing design.

### 9.1 Accessibility

Inspect and improve:

- Semantic HTML.
- Keyboard navigation.
- Visible focus indicators.
- Screen reader labels.
- Color contrast.
- Form labels.
- Accessible error messages.
- Modal focus behavior.
- Image alternative text.
- Heading hierarchy.
- Touch target sizes.
- Reduced-motion support.

Use WCAG 2.2 AA as the accessibility target.

### 9.2 Performance

Inspect actual runtime and build performance.

Investigate:

- Large JavaScript bundles.
- Unnecessary dependencies.
- Render-blocking resources.
- Excessive network requests.
- Unoptimized images.
- Layout shifts.
- Unnecessary re-rendering.
- Memory usage.
- Expensive animations.
- Main-thread blocking.
- Slow navigation.
- Poor mobile performance.

Implement appropriate optimizations.

Use:
- Correct image formats and responsive image sizes.
- Lazy loading where appropriate.
- Code splitting where beneficial.
- Efficient component rendering.
- Appropriate caching.
- Optimized asset delivery.
- Reduced unnecessary animation work.
- Graceful behavior on lower-powered devices.

Maintain smooth interactions on both low-end and high-end devices.

Do not remove meaningful animations simply to improve performance. Optimize them while retaining the existing visual design.

### 9.3 Measure performance

Run appropriate performance audits before and after optimization.

Record actual results using Lighthouse or equivalent tools.

Review:
- Performance.
- Accessibility.
- Best Practices.
- SEO.
- Core Web Vitals where measurable.

Do not describe performance as perfect based on visual inspection alone.

Report the measured results and any remaining limitations.

